script:post-response {
  if (res.body && res.body.id) {
    bru.setVar('sqlPipelineId', res.body.id);
  }
}

tests {
  test("Pipeline created", function() {
    const status = res.getStatus();
    // 201=created. This collection assumes a clean DB per run (CI uses an ephemeral stack); a 409
    // would mean a previous run leaked — fail loudly rather than carry a stale id downstream.
    expect(status).to.equal(201);
  });
}

docs {
  **SQL saga step 7** — Prerequisites: DataSource AVAILABLE (step 4a), DataSet created (step 5), POSTGIS DataSink created (step 6).

  Creates the pipeline attached to the dataset, modelled as a CORE intermediate-representation graph. The graph is the mandatory linear data flow `start → cron → dataSource → mapping → geoPersistence → end` (the cron trigger is wired in front of the pull source): the wired SQL source node (`entityId` = `sqlDataSourceId`) feeds the mapping, which feeds the wired POSTGIS sink node (`entityId` = `sqlDataSinkId`). The adapter derives source and sink per pipeline from the graph's `entityId`s, which must also appear in `dataSourceIds`/`dataSinkIds`; a graph without a wired source or sink node is rejected at deploy.

  The mapping node carries an inline `mappingConfig` whose `fields` use the **geoPoint** op:
  `"$.geo": { "op": "geoPoint", "lon": "$.lon", "lat": "$.lat" }`.
  The adapter compiles this to a WKT `POINT(<lon> <lat>)` literal that the `GEOMETRY(POINT,4326)` column parses on insert; `$.stationid` is a straight copy. `source`/`target` are versioned CORE URNs (opaque labels for the mapping parser); `$schema` and `positions` are accepted-but-ignored.

  When the dataset is released, the saga deploys this pipeline to the pipeline engine writing to the PostGIS sink.

  **Expected Response:** 201 Created, body contains `id`. A 409 means a previous run leaked (the test asserts a strict 201 and fails).

  **Post-condition:** Save `sqlPipelineId` from `body.id`.

  **System Impact:**

  | Layer | Effect |
  |---|---|
  | Backend | Pipeline record created in `portal_backend` DB linked to the dataset; DataSink `pipeline_id` set |
  | Config Adapter | — |
  | Pipeline engine | Pipeline is deployed only after the DATASET_CREATE saga runs (step 9) |
}
